\title{\code{\verb~|mask_first~} with a count of 2^63 or more gives an empty mask}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-15}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{compiler}
\tag{simd}
\meta{status}{closed}
\meta{completed}{2026-11-15}
\meta{created}{2026-11-15}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} closed by \pr{1038}.}
\p{\code{\verb~|core::simd::mask_first<M>(n)~} takes its counts as \code{\verb~|u64~} and is documented to set every lane when a count is past the extent. A count with the top bit set gives no lane instead: \code{\verb~|count(mask_first<Simd<[bool; 8]>>(n))~} is 8 for n = 2^63 - 1 and 0 for n = 2^63 and for n = 2^64 - 1. Found by executing \pr{1000} on x86-64, on AArch64 with NEON and on AArch64 with SVE, at every optimization level, see [ses-G5OI](ses-G5OI).}
\p{\strong{Cause.} The lowering casts the count to \code{\verb~|index~} and passes it to \code{\verb~|vector.create_mask~}, which reads its operands as signed: its lowering clamps with a signed minimum and compares signed on x86-64, and becomes the signed \code{\verb~|whilelt~} on SVE.}
\p{\strong{Fix, \pr{1038}.} A scalable mask of one dimension is built with \code{\verb~|llvm.intr.get.active.lane.mask~}, which compares unsigned and is one \code{\verb~|whilelo~} on SVE, where the signed \code{\verb~|whilelt~} was. A fixed mask, or one of several dimensions, keeps \code{\verb~|vector.create_mask~} and brings each count down to its extent with an unsigned minimum first; on x86-64 and NEON that replaces a signed clamp and costs nothing. A fixed mask does not use the intrinsic because it compares 64-bit lane indices: for 8 lanes of 32 bits it takes 11 instructions on AVX2 and 18 on NEON, where the clamp takes 8 and 13. A first version clamped scalable masks too, which put three instructions in front of \code{\verb~|whilelt~} on SVE. The documented use, \code{\verb~|mask_first(len - i)~} in a loop with i below len, never reaches such a count.}
